+# Send pickup reminders by SMS
+
+For whoever proposes or changes how larder reminds members to pick up an order. Pickup reminders go by SMS, not
+email, because a trial of email reminders in 2025 raised missed pickups and a missed pickup throws out food.
+
+## Context
+
+A missed pickup leaves a packed bag of fresh food that goes in the bin by closing time, so the channel a reminder
+goes by is judged by whether members see it in time. Between April and June 2025, larder sent pickup reminders by
+email instead of SMS: 34% of the reminder emails were never opened, and missed pickups rose from 6% to 13% of pantry
+orders. Many members share one phone in the family and rarely check email, but see an SMS. Larder switched back to
+SMS on July 1, 2025, and missed pickups were back to 6% by August. The question has come up three times since: two
+forces judge the options, the fraction of members who see the reminder in time, and its cost to larder.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Email**: free; lost because during the trial it was never opened by 34% of recipients, and missed pickups nearly
+  doubled.
+- **SMS**: costs larder 40 euros a month; chosen because it reaches members who share a phone and don't check email,
+  and measurably kept missed pickups at 6% rather than 13%.
+
+## Decision
+
+Pickup reminders go by SMS. The 2025 trial is the test any future proposal to change the channel must pass: it
+already ran email, under real conditions, and email cost missed pickups more than it saved.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Larder pays 40 euros a month for SMS instead of sending reminders for free. Missed pickups, and the food thrown out
+with them, stay at the lower rate SMS holds. A future proposal to drop SMS should account for the 2025 trial's result
+before larder repeats it.
